\f1\fs22 Sarah had instructed the engineering team to take the weekend off, but they worked around the clock anyway.\
Johnny\'92s only concern was there was no special chair for him in the Ironing Room anymore. Gone was the command-station-in-sofa that Johnny had demanded but which had nothing to do with him being a Star Trek fanboy, even if the franchise had lost its soul over the last century or so. Still, everyone preferred a Captain Kirk aesthetic over a Han Solo one; at least that\'92s what Johnny heard from the people he had hired, who were the only kind of people he had consulted on the matter. Although they did agree that the Stockwell man was a bit of a Chewbacca.\
There was an adequately comfortable chair behind an elegant white desk on an elevated dais at the back of the room. Johnny had eyed it off and sneered at it because it only had a couple of touch screens built into its inclining panels. Nothing about the desk said 
\f2\i Command
\f1\i0  or 
\f2\i Project Genius
\f1\i0 . 
\f2\i Tourist
\f1\i0  was more like it, Johnny thought.\
That was until Sarah walked in and the Stockwell man deferentially showed her to that very desk and the comfy chair behind it. Johnny was outraged. Why should Sarah be given special treatment and allowed to cool her heels while he was forced to stand and loiter over the central consoles alongside Quark and the Stockwell man. As if he was on their level.\
The Stockwell man brought Sarah a glass of water, a cup of tea and a Chocolate Monte. What the tax? Is he hitting on her now? Get in line, mate. And wait until she\'92s deported the kid, at least. Have some decency.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah thanked Chip for his kindnesses, then noticed Johnny staring at her like she\'92d nicked the last biscuit. So she popped the whole Chocolate Monte in her mouth and chewed. Johnny spun on his heels as if he\'92d been scandalised.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Sarah scanned the Ironing Room. The whole thing had been completely remodelled. For the better. Johnny\'92s old chair with all the doomsday buttons and toggles in the arms had gone. That was probably the scandal, she realised.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth0 The glow from the front wall dominated the otherwise dimly lit room, as well as the monitoring screen pair on each of the dozen or so desks. Sleek racks of lights and meters acted as partitions between the desks.  But there were no buttons, dials or toggles on any of them. No cables were tied to the wall. Neither were there any gaffed to the floor.\
Six different views of the new washing machine appeared in thinly bordered rectangles on the one integrated display on the front wall. Even the lowest metre to the floor displayed status badges, tickers, graphs, wave oscillation, though Sarah could not see all of it from where she sat. On a guess, she tapped the devices  integrated into the desk\'97her desk?\'97and quickly called up the same visualisations. She found the same washing machine renderings too. The front wall gave similar space to an elevation and a top view  of the  elliptical rail. Two more presented detailed realistic schematics of the two harnesses. Another pair glowed a quiet grey. Presumably they would light up when the show got underway.\
The Sages arrived and greeted everyone. As with earlier versions of the Ironing Room, they were afforded no allocated space, but Sarah knew they preferred to wander about and stand at the shoulder of this technician or that, as their whim took them.\
With the Sages in attendance, Dr Quark had the washing machine booted, initialised, and set in motion. It all seemed so stately to Sarah, for a time. It was faster than knitting, but not by much.\
\'934800 rpm, please,\'94 Dr Quark requested and Sarah was pleased with the acoustics.\
The left grey panels in the wall lit up with the target kebab speed in green. Two numbers in white and a slightly smaller font appeared underneath\'97the actual speeds of the accelerating kebabs. A graph underneath displayed their worms, presenting rotation speed over time. One was flat and green, the others white and approaching the green one.\
As the white numbers approached 4800 rpm they turned green, as did their worms. All three lines converged and became one.\
The right grey panel lit up and displayed representations of the washing machine articulators. One displayed the azimuth of the whole contraption. Another showed the angle of the plane of the rail. A third displayed both arms, each representing their angle variance from the plane of the rail.\
Sarah realised there were two more displays: two thin vertical bars that represented the rotation speeds of the secondary arms. Both seemed to have defaulted to 600rpm.\
\'93Ready for self-orientation, sir,\'94 a technician called while keeping her focus on her own touch screens.\
\'93Engage,\'94 Johnny ordered.\
Everyone turned and stared.\
\'93Hit it,\'94 Dr Quark said, and everyone went back to work.\
It took Johnny a few more seconds to realise he was not in charge. Yet.\
The Ironing Room held its breath. For fifteen seconds or so, nothing. Then another fifteen while everyone wondered if anything would happen.\
\'93We have kebab revolution compensation,\'94 a technician said.\
Two of the the numbers on the left wall panel began changing every second or so. The corresponding worms on the graph rose and fell, forming gentle hills and valleys compared to the flat control speed of 4800 rpm.\
Johnny smiled like a kid with a new puppy. Everyone else was solemnly professional, apart from the few who caught themselves holding their breath.\
The room watched the whole washing machine slew around its azimuth track while the elliptical rail tipped slowly in its cradle. It settled seventeen degrees west of north with the rail inclined almost twenty-six degrees from horizontal.\
A technician called out the numbers.\
One look up at Dr Quark. \'93Sir. In which direction is the CMB dipole?\'94\
Dr Quark looked at the vintage watch on his wrist and threw a pointed finger behind him. \'93That way.\'94\
The room laughed, with the exception of Johnny, Chip and Sarah. Chip wheeled on his heels. Sarah caught his eye. Chip lifted a chair in one hand, set it down next to Sarah, and stressed-tested it by planting his butt in it.\
\'93That freak is always right,\'94 Chip muttered.\
\'93What\'92s the dipole?\'94 Sarah asked.\
Chip narrowed his eyes at Sarah. \'93It\'92s not in your reports?\'94\
\'93No.\'94\
Chip exhaled. \'93Cosmic Microwave Background is the remnant radiation from the early universe.\'94\
\'93So the washing machine is pointing back at the origins of the universe?\'94\
Chip wobbled his head. \'93The universe didn\'92t just bang itself into existence on a park bench over there. The washing machine points along our motion relative to the background, and keeps tracking it over time. They\'92re all laughing about it now, but you should have seen them all swoon the first time it did that.\'94\
Sarah pulled a face. \'93Telescopes must do that all the time.\'94\
\'93The washing machine isn\'92t a telescope, and none of the poindexters expected that cosmological directions would be important for kebab motion. There\'92s no astronomical instrumentation or explicit programming for it to do that, but it does it anyway.\'94\
Sarah looked back to the front wall. Camera angles showed the two primary arms becoming more animated. The schematics of the arms\'92 orientations with respect to the rail\'92s steady plane showed them swivelling. As the arms travelled around the rail, they slowed and accelerated. To Sarah, one seemed to wait for the other to catch up, only for it to race away again. Then they seemed to shimmy on the rail, as if they were trying on a position for size. \
\'93They look like they\'92re dancing,\'94 Sarah said.\
Chip made a face like he hadn\'92t considered that, but agreed anyway.\
\'93More like acrobats,\'94 Sarah corrected, \'93swinging high and low as they go.\'94\
\'93Monkeys on a bar,\'94 Chip said.\
Sarah grinned. \'93Let\'92s call them monkeys, swinging around with a kebab in their hand.\'94\
Chip soured. \'93All your cutesy terminology is not commensurate with the gravity of what we\'92re doing here.\'94\
\'93Gravity, hah!\'94\
Little Clinton kicked her for that.\
Sarah watched the representations of the monkeys running and swinging and shimmying on the rail while spinning a crazy lunch box with a kebab in it, at much faster than baton-twirling speed.\
The technicians spoke in cool professional terms as Dr Quark ordered to increase the kebab-flipping speed. Up it went in increments until the kebabs reached a nominal 54,000 rpm. Sarah noticed the relative peaks and troughs of the two kebabs were sharper and deviated more from the nominal. Occasionally they flashed to orange then returned to green.\
The monkeys became more animated, moving faster and swinging in wider arcs above and below the rail. The rail rocked gently around its inclination. The whole washing machine might have been adjusting its position on the azimuth rail, no faster than the sun moving across the sky. Sarah tapped on her console; within two menus, she found the data. The washing machine was crabbing slowly left.\
One of the wall renderings stopped displaying a camera view of the washing machine. In its place, an animation appeared of the kebabs\'92 movements, without the distraction of the mechanisms propelling them.   Their paths curled around one another, and translucent surfaces formed between them, stretched and folded, then disappeared into new ones.\
Sarah closed her eyes, and visions came to her\'97of the kebabs flowing in something more than just the space in the Laundry. \
Little Clinton rolled and moved. Dog paddle, Sarah decided. Possibly getting into a breaststroke.\
She opened her eyes. Three monitors were approximating what she had just seen.\
The monkey swinging shimmy dance was becoming more frenetic.\
Dr Quark increased the speed of the flipping kababs. The primary arm monkeys adjusted; their dancing got jerkier. The worms on the chart became more angular, spent more time being orange, and occasionally flashed red. After the next increase, the monkeys\'92 movements appeared more urgent, as if they were trying to be in more than one place at once. They seemed to find somewhere they wanted to be, hung there for a moment, then tore themselves away. Each hesitation made the rail react.\
Sarah\'92s visions became sharper. She shook. She grasped the desk with both hands, and found herself breathing chaotically. Oddly, she felt as though Little Clinton was helping her to calm down.\
The worms representing the kebab flipping speed were mostly red, with flares of orange. Sarah thought she saw the elliptical rails shudder in places where the monkeys swung past.\
\'93Vibrations in the primary arms are exceeding tolerance,\'94 a technician announced.\
\'93Reduce speed,\'94 Dr Quark ordered.\
It made no difference.\
\'93Stop the test.\'94 The order sounded gentle and ancient.\
It came from Kleo. \'93You\'92re reducing it to pegs and planks.\'94\
Chip leapt to his feet, and regretted his weight coming down his ankles.\
Sarah closed her eyes and found the shapes waiting for her.\
Little Clinton kicked beneath her hand. Or perhaps he was clapping. Applause?\
The Laundry camera acquired a tremor.\
\'93Stop the test,\'94 Kleo repeated.\
Quark glanced at the wall. \'93Shutdown.\'94\
The green target worm began to fall. The red ones turned white and followed it down.\
The primary arms stopped being acrobatic. They slowed down to the top speed of a shopping trolley.\
The secondary arms slowed and became visible again.\
Little Clinton delivered one final kick.\
Sarah rubbed at her belly. \'93Everyone\'92s a critic.\'94
